Bug: a header deleted on disk keeps its references and its include edges

A header deleted between requests is never seen to change. In
removeReferencesOfChangedFiles() (src/rebuild.c) the test is
changeTime < contentTime, and a vanished file's content time reads as zero, so
its references stay. rescanChangedFiles() (src/projectstructure.c) has the same
blind spot, changedAfter(0, scanTime) is false, so its edges stay in the graph.
markMissingFilesAsDeleted() only looks at CUs, and only on the first request or a
config change. The includers are out of date (inputChangedAfter() counts zero as
a change) and are built, but their #include fails and nothing removes what is
already in header.h. It is not marked deleted, so the snapshot saves it again.

Likely fix: a vanished file is a change, for headers and CUs alike. It is
rescanned to no edges, its references are removed and it is marked deleted.
That is the "changed" line of the shape in doc/docs/20-insights.adoc, which
should include files that appeared or vanished. Files that appear are the other
half of the gap: nothing finds them between requests today, and
doc/docs/12-components.adoc ("Design boundary") accepts that. Directory
modification times, which move when an entry is added, removed or renamed, are
one way to find both.

Found by reading the code, confirmed by this test (Mac, session 4839a129,
2026-11-06).
